---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Quickstart"
  headings={[]}
  source="site/src/pages/docs/quickstart.astro"
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Edit model weights with Python</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Access named tensors, evaluate your changes, and save a standalone
      checkpoint.
    </p>
    <Code code={`python3 -m pip install 'wsm[editing]==0.1.0'`} lang="bash" />
    <Code
      code={`from wsm import WeightModel

model = WeightModel.load("/path/to/snapshot")
model.weights["model.norm.weight"][:16].mul_(0.95)
result = model.evaluate([{"prompt": "Say hello.", "target": "Hello!"}])
model.save("/path/to/checkpoint")`}
      lang="python"
    />
    <p>
      To run a file, define <code>edit(model)</code> and execute it with the
      CLI.
    </p>
    <p>
      Save the following as <code>edit.py</code>.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`def edit(model):
    model.weights["model.norm.weight"][:16].mul_(0.95)`}
      lang="python"
    />
    <p>
      Save a JSON array of prompt and target objects as <code>cases.json</code>.
    </p>
    <Code code={`[{"prompt": "Say hello.", "target": "Hello!"}]`} lang="json" />
    <Code
      code={`mkdir -p runs
wsm execute edit.py --model /path/to/snapshot --cases cases.json --output runs/edit`}
      lang="bash"
    />
    <p>
      The runner records baseline and candidate evaluations. You decide whether
      to keep the change. Python executes with your process privileges.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/docs/coding/">Read the Python editing guide</a> or{' '}
      <a href="/docs/quickstart/">Start building</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
